LIKEOS-INPUT-DEVICES(1)          User Commands         LIKEOS-INPUT-DEVICES(1)

NAME
       likeos-input-devices - keep the X server's touchpad configuration
       current

SYNOPSIS
       likeos-input-devices [-f] [-n] [-o FILE]

DESCRIPTION
       The X server here finds its input devices in its configuration, not
       by itself.  The keyboard (/dev/input/event0) and the mouse
       (/dev/input/event1) are named in /etc/X11/xorg.conf and its siblings;
       a touchpad's event node is numbered when it appears, so it cannot be.

       likeos-input-devices finds the touchpads the kernel reports (through
       libudev: event nodes tagged ID_INPUT_TOUCHPAD) and writes
       /etc/X11/xorg.conf.d/50-likeos-touchpads.conf, one InputDevice section
       per touchpad, driven by libinput and added to the server's layout with
       Option "AutoServerLayout".  On a machine without a touchpad it removes
       the file, and the server starts as it would without this program.
       The server reads the file when it starts.

       init starts it at boot (/etc/inittab) with -f: it writes the file,
       then rewrites it whenever an input device appears or goes away
       (/dev/devevents).  It runs as root, because the file is part of the
       configuration of the setuid X server.

       The touchpad settings, unless /etc/likeos/touchpad.conf says
       otherwise: tap to click and tap-and-drag on; two-finger scrolling
       (edge scrolling on a touchpad that cannot tell two fingers apart);
       natural scrolling off; on clickpads, libinput's default for the
       model, which on most clickpads is a click in the bottom right corner
       for the right button (a tap with two fingers is the right button
       with every click method); disable while typing on; adaptive pointer
       acceleration.  Palm and thumb detection, pinch and swipe gestures and
       the model corrections from libinput's quirks database always apply.

OPTIONS
       -f     Stay in the foreground and keep the file current (for init).
              SIGHUP rewrites it at once, for example after the settings
              file changed.

       -n     Print the configuration that would be written; change
              nothing.

       -o FILE
              Maintain FILE instead of the default.

FILES
       /etc/X11/xorg.conf.d/50-likeos-touchpads.conf
              The configuration written.  Do not edit it.

       /etc/likeos/touchpad.conf
              Settings, one KEY=VALUE per line, # for comments:

              Tapping, TappingDrag, TappingDragLock, NaturalScrolling,
              HorizontalScrolling, DisableWhileTyping, MiddleEmulation,
              LeftHanded
                     on or off

              ScrollMethod
                     twofinger, edge or none

              ClickMethod
                     buttonareas (the bottom of a clickpad is split into
                     left and right buttons), clickfinger (a click with two
                     fingers is the right button, with three the middle
                     one) or none

              AccelProfile
                     adaptive or flat

              AccelSpeed
                     -1.0 to 1.0

              After a change, run likeos-input-devices as root (or send the
              running one SIGHUP) and restart X.

EXAMPLES
       Show what the X server will be given:

              likeos-input-devices -n

       What libinput makes of the touchpad, and its events:

              libinput list-devices
              libinput debug-events --device /dev/input/event6

SEE ALSO
       devd(1), init(8)

